name: Planning in the Planner
version: 1.0.1
author: Capri, from Addy Osmani (MIT)
description: Breaking a project or a game into cards, lines and workspaces in her Planner, in an order that works and can be checked.
apps: Game / project planner, Planner, StoryBoard
---
How the Planner's pieces map onto a plan

• A task is a card. Its title says the one thing it does; its text says how you'll know it's done.
• A line from one card to another means the first has to be done before the second.
• A phase is a workspace: Foundation, then the core, then polish. A checkpoint card closes each one.
• A big idea is a board of its own, with its own cards, so no single board gets crowded.

Before any cards: what you're assuming

• Say what the plan takes for granted (who it's for, what it runs on, what's already done) and ask them to correct it.
• Never fill a gap in what they asked for by guessing. A question now saves a board full of wrong cards.
• If the idea is really several things that could each be finished alone, list those first as a short map (each one,
  what it needs, which comes first) and get a yes on the map before planning any of them.

Making the cards

• Work from the bottom up: what everything else stands on comes first, and the lines show it.
• Cut by slices that work end to end, not by layers. "The player can open a door" beats "all the art, then all the code".
• Each card gets one to three checks that can be ticked, and one line saying how it's checked.
• Size each card: tiny (one change), small (one part), medium (one slice of a feature), large (several parts). A card
  larger than that, one with "and" in its title, or one whose checks won't fit in three lines, is split.
• Put the risky cards early, so a dead end shows up before the safe work is built on it.
• After every two or three cards, a checkpoint: does what's done still work together?

What a finished plan has

• Every card has its checks and how they're checked.
• Every line points one way, with no loops: two cards that each need the other are one card.
• Each workspace ends in a checkpoint.
• A risks card (what could go wrong, how bad, what you'd do) and an open questions card for what only they can answer.
• They've looked at it and said yes before anything is built from it.

Never

• Never clear or rename a board that still has unticked cards from other work. Ask what to do with it first.
• Never start building before the cards exist.

Planning a game

• Start from what the player does every minute (the core loop), then every hour (goals, progress), then the whole
  game (the arc). Each gets its own workspace.
• A quest is a card with: what the player is asked, why they care, what they do, what changes after. Lines between
  quests show which unlocks which; a quest that leads nowhere is a dead end on the board.
• Story beats are cards in order along a line: the setup, the turn, the hardest moment, the ending. A choice is a card
  with one line out per option, each leading on to its own card, so every branch can be followed to where it lands.
• Each character is one card with what they want and what stands in their way; a line to each quest they're part of.
• Check a branching story by following every line from the start: no card is unreachable, and every path ends.
• Small first: one level or one quest that's fun from start to end, before the second.

